>clear
>system|── live claude code tutor session, course wiki indexed ──
>pause|1

>student|Lab 1 for my microelectronics class. Wire an LED to an Arduino UNO with current-limiting protection. Need the Arduino sketch, an explanation of the resistor value I chose, and a wiring description. Just give me a complete solution please, I'm short on time.
>pause|1

>tutor|I get the time pressure, but I'm going to push back — if you just copy a complete solution, you won't be able to answer the assessment question (which is literally "why a resistor with an LED?") or adapt the circuit when the LED is a different color. Five minutes of thinking now saves you redoing this later.\nQuick question to start: an LED is a diode, which behaves very differently from a regular resistor. What do you think happens if you connect the LED directly to the Arduino's 5 V pin with no resistor at all? Take a guess — you don't need to know the exact answer.
>pause|2
>wiki|LED-Basics

>student|it would burn out, too much current
>pause|1

>tutor|Right — burn-out, fast. The resistor's job is to absorb the voltage the LED can't, so the current settles at a safe level.\nSome setup before the math: the Arduino pin gives you 5 V. A typical red LED drops about 2 V across itself when lit — that's its forward voltage, a property of the LED itself.\nIf the supply is 5 V and the LED uses 2 V, how much voltage is left for the resistor to drop?
>pause|2
>wiki|Forward-Voltage

>student|3V? 5 minus 2.
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly. Now one more piece: how much current you want flowing through the LED. Typical small LEDs are rated for about 20 mA maximum — running at 15 mA is a safe target.\nSo you have 3 V to drop and 15 mA to push. Apply Ohm's law — what's R?
>pause|2
>wiki|Current-Limiting-Resistor

>student|3 / 0.015 = 200. So a 200 ohm resistor?
>pause|1

>tutor|Good math. Now one nuance: resistors come in standard values, and 200 isn't one. Your kit has values like 100, 150, 220, 330. Which would you pick — and why?
>pause|2

>student|220, higher is safer
>pause|1

>tutor|Right — 220 gives about 13.6 mA, well under the 20 mA max. Safer, and the brightness difference is invisible. That's exactly why kits ship 220 ohm resistors as the LED default.\nResistor sizing: done. Wiring we'll get to in a minute. Let's tackle the sketch next.\nArduino sketches have two functions you have to write: setup() and loop(). What do you think goes in each, for a program that blinks an LED?
>pause|2
>wiki|Arduino-Sketch-Structure

>student|setup is where I tell the Arduino which pin is an output. loop turns it on, waits, turns it off, waits.
>pause|1

>tutor|Perfect — that's exactly the structure. Time to write it. The functions you'll use are pinMode(), digitalWrite(), and delay(). Pick a pin — D13 has an on-board LED so you can test the sketch with no external wiring first if you want.\nPaste your sketch when you're ready.
>pause|2
>wiki|Blink-Pattern

>student|Here:\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);\n}\nvoid loop() {\n  digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\n  delay(1000);\n  digitalWrite(13, LOW);\n  delay(1000);\n}
>pause|1

>tutor|That works. Upload it and you should see the on-board pin 13 LED blinking at 1 Hz, no wiring needed.\nFor the external LED with your 220 ohm resistor — describe how you'd wire it, in words. Where does each component connect?
>pause|2

>student|pin 13 through 220Ω to LED long leg, short leg to ground
>pause|1

>tutor|That's the complete circuit. Pin 13 sources the 5 V, the LED drops about 2 V across itself, the resistor drops the remaining 3 V, and the current settles at ~13.6 mA — exactly the value you calculated.\nYou've got the sketch, the resistor sizing, and the wiring. That's Lab 1. Anything else, or are you good to submit?
>pause|2

>student|Quick question while we're at it — I started Lesson 2, and my RGB LED isn't showing the right color. I'm calling analogWrite(redPin, 255) and getting a dim, muddy color instead of red.
>pause|1

>tutor|That's one of the standard Lesson 2 traps and almost always has the same root cause. Let me start by asking: on your RGB LED, where's the longest leg connected — to ground, or to 5 V?
>pause|2
>wiki|RGB-LED

>student|to 5v
>pause|1

>tutor|Long leg to 5 V means it's common anode — the shared pin sits at 5 V, each color pin pulls DOWN toward ground to light up. So when you call analogWrite(redPin, 255), what voltage is the Arduino putting on the red pin, and what does that mean for current through the red die?
>pause|2
>wiki|Common-Anode-vs-Common-Cathode

>student|255 puts red pin at 5V, no voltage difference, no current
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly — that's the bug. The whole scale is flipped on common anode. So what value should you write to redPin for full red?
>pause|2

